<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of the Suebi</h1><p class="st-meta">409 CE – 585 CE · capital Bracara Augusta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Germanic kingdom that ruled Gallaecia from Braga for over 170 years, one of the first barbarian realms in the former Roman West.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/suebi">Suebi</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 406 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/alans">Alans</a> and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> in 409. In 411 they received lands in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gallaecia">Gallaecia</a> under their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermeric">Hermeric</a>. After the Vandals left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 429, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rechila">Rechila</a> expanded south, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta">Emerita Augusta</a> in 439 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> in 441.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom was centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Bracara Augusta</a> and ruled a Hispano-Roman majority through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and local elites. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rechiar">Rechiar</a> became the first Catholic Germanic king around 448 and minted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins, but was defeated and killed by the Visigoth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-ii-king-of-the-visigoths">Theodoric II</a> in 456. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/suebi">Suebi</a> later turned Arian, then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-of-braga">Martin of Braga's</a> influence around 560. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/councils-of-braga">Councils of Braga</a> of 561 and 572 organised the church.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miro-king-of-the-suebi">Miro</a> was drawn into Visigothic conflicts and died in 583. In 585 the Visigothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liuvigild">Leovigild</a> overthrew the usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andeca">Andeca</a> and annexed the kingdom, ending Suebic rule. Its territory shaped the later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia">Kingdom of Galicia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-the-suebi.jpg" alt="Kingdom of the Suebi" width="320" height="275"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Vigo (talk · contribs) · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reino_suevo_em_448-pt.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>409 CE – 585 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bracara Augusta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hermeric, Rechiar, Miro</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Arianism, then Nicene Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Portugal, Spain</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Suebic Kingdom of Galicia, Regnum Suevorum</dd></dl></aside></div>
